From d014ae83fce42d24ab0a7100b14ee894ad74cdfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W1nkelm4nn <mrmarkap61@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:15:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: Read zeroed textures through null descriptors

Every image and texture access through a descriptor that may be null was
wrapped in a branch that skips it and yields zero. In shaders reading
thousands of textures the branches dominate the MSL: Horizon Zero Dawn's
largest compute shader has 5423 of them on one descriptor, and Metal's
compiler took 14.4 s for it, 2.3 s without any null handling.

The device now has zeroed 1x1 textures of every texture type, for float,
int and uint, and the root descriptor table the address of their resource
IDs. Texel reads (samples without depth comparison, fetches, gathers and
image loads) select that address for a null descriptor and need no
branch. Size queries, writes, atomics, depth comparisons, sparse and
multisampled accesses keep the branch. A zeroed texture differs from a
null descriptor only where the sampler returns its border color.

This replaces MESA_KK_NULL_IMAGE_SELECT, which read through the null
resource ID and selected zero: it compiled faster, but slowed Medieval
Dynasty from 22 to 15-20 fps. MESA_KK_NULL_TEXTURES=0 branches as before.

Generated-by: LLM
